<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alice Seeley Harris</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1870">1870</a>–<a href="/years/1970">1970</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alice Seeley Harris was an English Baptist missionary in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a> whose photographs of mutilated Congolese, taken from 1904 at the Baringa mission, became key evidence in the campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii">Leopold II</a>'s regime. With her husband John Harris she showed them at meetings across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-reform-association">Congo Reform Association</a>, and she is seen as a pioneer of humanitarian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/photography">photography</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alice-seeley-harris.jpg" alt="Alice Seeley Harris" width="200" height="268" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alice_Seeley_Harris_(20e_eeuw).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alice Seeley was born in Malmesbury on 24 May 1870, the daughter of Aldred and Caroline Seeley. In 1889, aged 19, she entered the Civil Service and was later appointed to the Accountant General's office of the General Post Office in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. In her spare time she helped with Frederick Brotherton Meyer's mission work at Regent's Park Chapel and Christ Church, Lambeth, and at missionary classes she met John Harris. Accepted for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a> in 1897, she trained at Doric Lodge, the college of the Regions Beyond Missionary Union, and married John on 6 May 1898.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Four days after the wedding the couple sailed for the Congo with the Congo-Balolo Mission, arriving on 4 August 1898. They served at Ikau, near the Lulonga River, until 1901, and then at Baringa on the Maringa River until 1905. Alice taught the local children reading and Bible stories, but her chief work was photographing the injuries inflicted by the agents and soldiers of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii">Leopold II</a>, whose regime drove the collection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> by whipping, hostage-taking, rape, murder and the burning of villages. In 1904 a man named Nsala came to the mission carrying the severed hand and foot of his daughter, killed with his wife by sentries of the Abir Congo Company, and she persuaded him to be photographed with them.</p><p>Her pictures appeared first in the mission magazine Regions Beyond, then in 1904 in the pamphlet Congo Slavery and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/e-d-morel">E. D. Morel</a>'s King Leopold's rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. From 1906 the Harrises worked for Morel's Congo Reform Association, showing her images by magic lantern at 200 meetings in 49 cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. In 1910 they became joint organising secretaries of the Anti-Slavery and Aborigines' Protection Society, and after revisiting the Congo in 1911 and 1912 they published Present Conditions in the Congo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>She became Lady Harris when her husband was knighted in 1933, a title she disliked, and helped him at the Society until his death in 1940. She lived for many years in Frome and died at Guildford on 24 November 1970, aged 100. In 2014 the International Slavery Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a> held an exhibition of her photographs, Brutal Exposure: the Congo, and in 2017 a plaque was unveiled at Merchants Barton in Frome, where she had lived as a girl.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alice Seeley Harris” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…George Washington Williams denounced Leopold’s rule in an open letter in 1890; missionaries such as William Sheppard and <b>Alice Seeley Harris</b>, whose photographs of mutilated people were shown at meetings across Britain and America, publicised the atrocities; the British consul Roger Casement’s report of 1904 gave them official…</blockquote><a href="/empire/congo-free-state">Congo Free State · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Malmesbury, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Guildford, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.59,-2.1&amp;year=1935" aria-label="Malmesbury on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4503 379 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Alice Seeley Harris"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4953 649h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4953 649h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.59,-2.1&amp;year=1935">What was here in 1935? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
